{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The Cmd+K box that made you reach for the mouse anyway</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team shipped a Cmd+K command palette: press the shortcut, a search box pops up, type to filter a list of actions. It looked exactly like the ones in every tool their users already loved. Then support tickets came in — "the arrow keys don't do anything," "I have to click the result, pressing Enter does nothing," "when I close it, my cursor is gone and I don't know where focus went."
    </p>
    <p>
      The box searched. That was it. Everything past "type text, see filtered list" required a mouse — which meant it wasn't actually a command palette, it was a search input wearing a command palette's keyboard shortcut.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The whole value proposition of a command palette is "never touch the mouse." A search box that filters but doesn't support arrow-key navigation, Enter-to-select, or predictable focus handling fails at the one job that justifies its existence — it's slower than the menu it's supposedly replacing, because now the user has to switch input modes mid-task.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a focus-management problem wearing a search-UX costume. The filtering logic was the easy 20%; arrow-key navigation, roving <code>aria-activedescendant</code>, a focus trap while the palette is open, and focus restoration on close are the 80% that actually makes it usable without a pointer — and it's exactly the part that's invisible in a screenshot or a quick demo, so it's the part that gets skipped under deadline pressure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Keyboard-first means the keyboard drives everything, not just typing</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A real command palette needs three things working together: arrow keys move a visible "selected" highlight through the results, Enter activates whatever's highlighted, and Escape closes the palette and returns focus to wherever it was before — no clicking required at any step.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Under the hood, the highlighted item usually isn't given real DOM focus on every arrow press (that would fire a wall of focus events and confuse assistive tech) — instead the input keeps DOM focus the whole time, and <code>aria-activedescendant</code> on the input points at the id of the currently-highlighted option, a pattern from the ARIA combobox spec. Visually it looks like focus moved; structurally the input never let go.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Two failure modes compound if you skip the accessibility layer: a keyboard trap (Tab can escape the palette into the page behind it while it's still open, or never find its way back in) and a lost-focus bug on close (the palette unmounts and focus silently falls back to <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>, so the next Tab press starts from the top of the page instead of where the user was). Both are functionally invisible to a sighted mouse user clicking through a demo, and both make the feature unusable for anyone navigating by keyboard alone.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The palette that ejected users into the page behind it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A B2B dashboard's command palette was built by wiring an existing autocomplete input to a global keyboard shortcut. It filtered fine. But because it was never designed as a modal overlay, pressing Tab while it was open moved focus straight into the dashboard sidebar underneath it — the palette stayed visually open, but keyboard input now controlled a different part of the page entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Power users, the exact audience the feature was built for, hit this constantly, because they were the ones actually using the keyboard instead of clicking through.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team added a real focus trap: while the palette is open, Tab and Shift+Tab cycle only among elements inside it (the input and, once results render, nothing else needs to be independently tabbable since arrow keys handle navigation). They also stored the previously-focused element before opening the palette and explicitly called <code>.focus()</code> on it when the palette closed, instead of letting focus fall wherever the browser's default landed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also fixed a second-order bug the trap exposed: closing the palette via a result selection (not just Escape) needs the same focus-restoration path as an explicit cancel, or the "happy path" of actually using the palette successfully is the one case where focus gets lost. Every exit route — Escape, click-outside, selecting a result, even the palette triggering a route change — has to funnel through the same restore-focus step, or you fix the bug for the exit path you tested and reintroduce it on the one you didn't.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

